August 2013 an aerial photograph taken  by an Australian government survey team  reveals something unusual in a remote  patch of the outback a huge pattern of  bright red pock marks scarring the  landscape for hundreds of square miles I  get kind of creeped out when I see  images of pustules and stuff and that's  kind of what it looks like right here  just really a bad case of acne or  something the photograph is taken near  the remote town of Newman in the Pilbara  a sparsely populated desert region of  Western Australia so you've got a lot of  green and then all these like very  evenly spaced brown spots they're just  so evenly spaced that's the thing that's  really remarkable so what's really going  on here the Outback makes up around 70  percent of Australia's 2.9 million  square mile and mass but these red pock  marks appear only in the Pilbara region  this image is fascinating it's something  like I've never seen before  Jetson thinks that something much more  mind-boggling is going on he believes  that the plants are organizing  themselves and you see that the plants  are arranging themselves by  self-organization to maximize water  access and to grow better gets in has a  theory that the plants have evolved to  grow in circles as an adaptation to  survive in such a dry desert in arid  environments when water is not enough to  sustain a complete coverage with  vegetation plants create patterns and  these plants are simply forming a circle  in order to maximize access to water the  soil insanity circles actually helps  conduct moisture out to the plants on  the outside as if some higher  intelligence is directing them the  plants arrange themselves to form large  circular catchment areas these act as  rain collectors which then direct any  water back towards the plants  the smart plant theory is still unproven  but it could offer an answer to the  mysterious circle seen in two deserts  more than 6000 miles apart fairy circles  here in the Pilbara just another example  of how you know nature responds to the  to the climate it's got plants are  smarter than you think
 

